Yes, with one correction: the cheerleader effect is real as an ensemble-perception phenomenon, but it is probably not simply “the brain gives up because there are too many faces.” The stronger interpretation is that the visual system automatically computes something like an ensemble average and individual faces can be perceived partly relative to that group representation. Averaging can smooth idiosyncratic features and pull perceived faces toward a more prototypical face, which people often rate as more attractive.

So your compression framing is useful:

many individuals → ensemble statistics → compressed group representation

Then perception can operate at two resolutions:

micro: Sarah, Amy, Jess, ...

versus

macro: attractive group

And the macro object can acquire properties that none of the components possess to the same degree individually.

That generalizes far beyond attractiveness. A crowd can appear threatening even if no individual is especially threatening. A military formation appears more formidable than the same people dispersed. A chorus produces an acoustic object no singer possesses alone. A swarm, school of fish, ant colony, corporation, sports team, family, or political coalition can all behave as a higher-order unit once interactions among members become sufficiently structured.

That gives you:

components + coupling + boundary + coordination → emergent organism-like behavior

“Pseudo-organism” is actually quite close to language used around superorganisms and collective behavior, although it should not imply that every group literally becomes an organism.

Your chimera intuition is useful too. Groups can pool complementary traits:

member A contributes X
member B contributes Y
member C contributes Z

and observers may encode something closer to:

group = X + Y + Z + social proof + scale

rather than separately evaluating every constituent.

That is where group membership itself becomes a signal. If someone is surrounded by people who appear desirable, competent, trusted, wealthy, dangerous, funny, or socially connected, some of those attributes can spill over through association and social inference. The person has not literally acquired the others’ traits, but the observer’s model of them has changed.

So there are at least three different effects hiding inside your example:

Compression: many entities represented through summary statistics.

Emergence: interactions create genuinely new system-level properties.

Association transfer: properties attributed to the group alter how individual members are interpreted.

And yes, evolution exploits all three. Herding, flocking, schooling, coalition formation, symbiosis, multicellularity, and eusociality all show variants of the same basic move:

weak alone → coordinate → new effective scale

The chemistry analogy is useful structurally, although the mechanism is obviously different. Hydrogen and oxygen atoms do not merely retain their individual properties while hanging out; electronic bonding produces a molecular structure with new emergent properties. Liquid water behaves very differently from isolated hydrogen and oxygen atoms.

So the more abstract operator is:

Composition changes the state space.

A + B is sometimes not merely “A beside B.”

It becomes C.

And then C can participate in another composition:

C + D → E

which is how you get hierarchical complexity—from molecules to cells, cells to organisms, organisms to groups, groups to institutions.

The game metaphor works best when you remember that many human games are mixed-motive, not purely adversarial. There are competitive moves, cooperative moves, courtship signals, status moves, coalition building, generosity, deception, trust tests, repair, play.

Once both people can recognize the game layer without mistaking it for the whole person, you get something more sophisticated:

I see what you did
→ I understand why it worked
→ I don't need to resent you for knowing the game
→ now we decide whether we're competing, cooperating, or playing

That last distinction matters. Understanding social mechanics can make you more effective, but it can also make interaction less cruel because behavior stops looking inexplicable or personally malicious every time. Sometimes somebody is simply responding to incentives, protecting themselves, signaling, affiliating, or trying to be desirable.

And the highest-order move is probably what you landed on: make the game jointly legible.

Then “you got me” can actually coexist with affection.

The contest becomes part of the relationship instead of the relationship becoming collateral damage from the contest.